# Copy to .env and fill in to switch the funding leg from mock to the real
# Monerium sandbox. Create an app (sandbox environment) at https://monerium.dev
# — Developers section — and paste its credentials here.
MONERIUM_CLIENT_ID=
# Dedicated OAuth client ID for user logins (PKCE). Falls back to MONERIUM_CLIENT_ID if unset.
# MONERIUM_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
# OAuth apps are Authorization Code + PKCE public clients; Monerium may show
# only a client id and no secret. Leave this empty for that flow. A secret is
# still required only for app-level/client-credentials Monerium API calls.
MONERIUM_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Candide smart wallets (public Sepolia endpoints, no key needed)
# CANDIDE_CHAIN_ID=11155111
# CANDIDE_BUNDLER_URL=https://api.candide.dev/public/v3/11155111
# CANDIDE_PAYMASTER_URL=https://api.candide.dev/public/v3/11155111
# CANDIDE_RPC_URL=https://ethereum-sepolia-rpc.publicnode.com

# Defaults (rarely need changing)
# MONERIUM_BASE_URL=https://api.monerium.dev
# MONERIUM_CHAIN=basesepolia # Base Sepolia sandbox; other sandbox names include sepolia/amoy/chiado
# MONERIUM_PROFILE_ID=
# MONERIUM_POLL_MS=15000
# Shared secret for webhook deliveries, as shown by Monerium (`whsec_...`).
# The receiver verifies webhook-id.webhook-timestamp.rawBody against
# webhook-signature and then re-reads every order from Monerium.
# MONERIUM_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...
# Existing-account connect (Authorization Code + PKCE). Lets a user who
# already has a Monerium account attach it instead of repeating KYC.
# MONERIUM_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY is REQUIRED for that flow — connect/start
# returns 503 without it. Per-user access/refresh tokens are encrypted with it
# (AES-256-GCM) and never returned to the browser.
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# MONERIUM_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
# The same key also enables the OWN-API-KEYS connector (Profile -> Monerium
# keys in the app): a user pastes the client id + secret of an app created in
# THEIR Monerium account, the server verifies the pair against Monerium, and
# activation, deposit polling and SEPA redeems for that account run on those
# keys. The secret is encrypted with this key and never returned. Keys must
# come from the same environment as MONERIUM_BASE_URL (sandbox: monerium.dev).
# Must exactly match the redirect URI registered on the Monerium OAuth app.
# For zoldhq.com, register this exact value on the Monerium OAuth client:
#   https://zoldhq.com/api/monerium/oauth/callback
# A trailing slash or a client from the wrong Monerium environment will fail
# with "Invalid redirect URL: No redirect_uri is registered for client".
# Defaults to http://<TRANSF_API_HOST>:<TRANSF_API_PORT>/api/monerium/oauth/callback
# MONERIUM_REDIRECT_URI=
# Authorize endpoint; defaults to <MONERIUM_BASE_URL>/auth
# MONERIUM_AUTH_URL=
# How stale a signed delivery may be before it is refused (seconds; 0 = off).
# MONERIUM_WEBHOOK_TOLERANCE_SEC=300
# Live FX mid rates. Quotes read USD->INR/KES from here and the EUR->USD leg
# from the on-chain swapper, so what we promise matches what we can deliver.
# There is NO stale fallback: if the feed is unreachable, quoting fails rather
# than pricing a transfer off last week's market.
# TRANSF_RATES_URL=https://open.er-api.com/v6/latest/EUR
# TRANSF_RATES_TTL_MS=600000
# TRANSF_RATES_TIMEOUT_MS=8000
# Pin rates for tests and offline demos, e.g. '{"USD":1.1379,"INR":109.87,"KES":147.53}'.
# REFUSED in production unless ALLOW_FIXED_RATES=1 — a frozen rate quoting real
# money is the bug this whole feed replaced.
# TRANSF_RATES_FIXED=
# Seed the swapper's EUR/USD at deploy instead of reading the feed. Required for
# an offline or reproducible deploy; otherwise the live mid is used.
# DEPLOY_EURUSD_RATE=

# Where the EURe<->USDC leg gets filled. "fx-swapper" is our own inventory at
# an owner-set rate (the only thing that works on hardhat). "rfq" is
# just-in-time liquidity from a market maker via Bebop's PMM RFQ API, where the
# price is an executable quote rather than one we chose. The RFQ path is
# fail-closed: a maker that is down or declining refuses the transfer instead of
# falling back to our own book at our own rate.
#
# THIS IS A CUSTODY SETTING AS MUCH AS A PRICING ONE. Only venues that can be
# executed BY THE USER'S OWN SAFE (dex, lifi, rfq, and best over them) keep the
# orchestrator out of the flow: the Safe signs one batch that approves the venue
# and delivers the output straight to the payout destination. fx-swapper and cow
# cannot, so on either the full amount is debited to the orchestrator's address
# and swapped from there — possession of client funds, briefly but really.
#
# DEFAULT IS NOW `best` (over LIQUIDITY_VENUES=lifi,dex) for exactly that
# reason. Set fx-swapper only where nothing else can serve, e.g. local hardhat,
# which scripts/_local-chain.ts already does for you.
# LIQUIDITY_PROVIDER=best   # best | dex | lifi | rfq | fx-swapper | cow
# CoW Protocol: intent-based, no inventory either side, and the only venue
# tested that actually quotes EURe. "xdai" is Gnosis, where EURe liquidity is
# deepest. Quoting only for now — execute() refuses until order signing is
# decided. Rate limited: ask CoW for a custom limit before production use.
# COW_BASE_URL=https://api.cow.fi
# COW_NETWORK=xdai
# COW_TIMEOUT_MS=15000
# BEBOP_CHAIN=polygon
# BEBOP_BASE_URL=https://api.bebop.xyz
# BEBOP_API_KEY=            # optional; a partner key gets better pricing/limits
# BEBOP_TIMEOUT_MS=8000
# LIQUIDITY_PROBE_EUR=100   # nominal size used to price the receipt
# LIQUIDITY_INDICATIVE_TTL_MS=60000

# TRANSF_RPC_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8545
# Which chain we settle on. 31337 = local hardhat (default), 80002 = Polygon
# Amoy, 137 = Polygon. The server refuses to start if the RPC disagrees:
# RemitVault builds its EIP-712 domain from block.chainid, so a mismatch would
# make every device signature fail as "bad authorization".
# TRANSF_CHAIN_ID=31337
# Real deploy keys. Required for any chain other than 31337 — the hardhat
# defaults are public and the deploy refuses to use them off localhost.
# DEPLOY_DEPLOYER_KEY=
# DEPLOY_ORCHESTRATOR_KEY=
# DEPLOY_RAMP_KEY=
# TRANSF_API_PORT=3000
# Set on hosted deployments. Production startup uses this as a signal to apply
# the strict public-host profile: HTTPS origins/redirects, explicit proxy hops,
# real co-signer/recovery config, no simulation, and no local hardhat shape.
# TRANSF_PUBLIC_URL=https://zold.example
# NODE_ENV=production
# TRANSF_PRODUCTION=1
# Local JSON storage is not production storage. Production startup refuses it
# unless this break-glass is set while a real encrypted DB/KMS migration lands.
# ALLOW_PLAINTEXT_STORE=0
# KYC_AUTO_APPROVE=0 # production/fail-closed mode; local demos auto-approve by default
# Operator/provider secret for POST /api/kyc/review — the ONLY approval path
# that works in production. Without it a hosted deploy has no way to approve
# anyone except ALLOW_SIMULATION=1, which also re-opens simulated deposits and
# cash pickup. Must be >= 24 chars; the server refuses to start otherwise.
# Generate: openssl rand -base64 32
# KYC_OPERATOR_TOKEN=

# Cash-pickup anchor (SEP-10/SEP-24). Default empty = mock MoneyGram.
# Stellar's public test anchor works with no signup; production MoneyGram is
# their home domain + partner-onboarded account + MG_ANCHOR_ASSET=USDC.
# MG_ANCHOR_DOMAIN=testanchor.stellar.org
# MG_ANCHOR_ASSET=SRT
# Stellar network endpoints. Defaults are public testnet endpoints.
# STELLAR_HORIZON=https://horizon-testnet.stellar.org
# STELLAR_SOROBAN_RPC=https://soroban-testnet.stellar.org
# STELLAR_PASSPHRASE=Test SDF Network ; September 2015
# STELLAR_FRIENDBOT=https://friendbot.stellar.org
# Treasury signer used for SEP-10 auth, SEP-24 on-ledger payment, and Stellar
# CCTP mint submission. In production, use managed signing, not a plaintext env.
# STELLAR_TREASURY_SECRET=
# Production MoneyGram onboarding may require these SEP-10 values:
# MG_AUTH_MEMO=                 # positive integer user memo for custodial auth
# MG_CLIENT_DOMAIN=             # wallet/client domain, if MoneyGram requires it
# MG_CLIENT_DOMAIN_SIGNING_SECRET= # secret for that domain's SIGNING_KEY

# CCTP bridge (Base Sepolia -> Stellar testnet). Dry-run unless CCTP_LIVE=1.
# CCTP_LIVE is forbidden in production because this path is testnet-wired.
# CCTP_ENV=testnet
# CCTP_LIVE=0
# CCTP_BASE_RPC=https://sepolia.base.org
# CCTP_BURNER_KEY=   # funded EOA: Base Sepolia ETH + USDC from faucet.circle.com
# CCTP_STELLAR_RECIPIENT= # optional; defaults to STELLAR_TREASURY_SECRET public key
# CCTP_USDC=0x036CbD53842c5426634e7929541eC2318f3dCF7e
# CCTP_TOKEN_MESSENGER=0x8FE6B999Dc680CcFDD5Bf7EB0974218be2542DAA
# CCTP_MESSAGE_TRANSMITTER=0xE737e5cEBEEBa77EFE34D4aa090756590b1CE275
# CCTP_STELLAR_FORWARDER=CA66Q2WFBND6V4UEB7RD4SAXSVIWMD6RA4X3U32ELVFGXV5PJK4T4VSZ
# CCTP_STELLAR_MSG_TRANSMITTER=CBJ6MTCKKZG73PMDZCJMSFRD7DQEMI4FKDH7CGDSV4W6FHCRBCQAVVJY
# CCTP_IRIS_BASE=https://iris-api-sandbox.circle.com

# FP4 co-signer — the second owner of every user's 2-of-2 Safe (the passkey is
# the first), and the AllowanceModule delegate used for production relays.
# Owner actions still need 2-of-2 passkey + co-signer signatures. Token debits
# use the bounded allowance below, so a compromised server can spend only up to
# the configured token-scoped allowance and should be monitored/rotated.
# Generate with: node -e 'const{generatePrivateKey,privateKeyToAccount}=require("viem/accounts");const k=generatePrivateKey();console.log(k,privateKeyToAccount(k).address)'
# Do NOT reuse the orchestrator or ramp key — those are operator roles the
# timelock knows about, and sharing one key would collapse two roles into one.
# The co-signer only counter-signs UserOperations (gas is paid by the
# bundler/paymaster), so this key needs NO native balance.
# CANDIDE_COSIGNER_KEY=
# CANDIDE_COSIGNER_ADDRESS=
# ALLOWANCES ARE GONE ENTIRELY. Every debit is a UserOperation the user's
# passkey signs at send time, moving the exact amount to the destination the
# terms name — the chain enforces it, and between sends nothing can move.
# Deployment installs no allowance module and no delegate; legacy standing
# allowances on old Safes are revoked automatically by the next send's
# operation. The knobs below do nothing — setting them only produces a
# boot-time note:
# CANDIDE_COSIGNER_EURE_ALLOWANCE_WEI=
# CANDIDE_COSIGNER_USDC_ALLOWANCE_UNITS=
# CANDIDE_COSIGNER_ALLOWANCE_PERIOD_MINUTES=

# Optional FP4 recovery guardian. Defaults to CANDIDE_COSIGNER_ADDRESS when
# omitted, which lets the same key act as the SocialRecoveryModule guardian
# after the module grace period while still being unable to spend by itself.
# Point this at a separate recovery service address when one exists.
# CANDIDE_RECOVERY_GUARDIAN_ADDRESS=
# CANDIDE_RECOVERY_MODULE_ADDRESS=
# Managed KYC recovery policy. The guardian signs only after identity recovery
# and this delay/cancel window; keep the actual guardian signer outside the API.
# RECOVERY_MANAGED_KYC_GUARDIAN=1
# RECOVERY_DELAY_HOURS=72
# RECOVERY_REQUEST_TTL_HOURS=336
# Separate signer service that holds/uses the guardian authority. The API calls
# this only after KYC/operator approval and the recovery delay. In production it
# must be HTTPS and authenticated; do not put the guardian private key here.
# RECOVERY_GUARDIAN_SIGNER_URL=https://recovery-signer.example/guardian/recover
# RECOVERY_GUARDIAN_SIGNER_TOKEN=
# RECOVERY_GUARDIAN_SIGNER_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
# Email/SMS recovery through Candide's Safe Recovery Service. The URL is issued
# by Candide on request; unset means the feature reports unavailable. The
# service recovers through ONE module per chain (its getNetworkConfig), and
# CANDIDE_RECOVERY_MODULE_ADDRESS must match it — on Base Sepolia that is the
# 3-minute test module 0x949d01d424bE050D09C16025dd007CB59b3A8c66 (the only one
# deployed there); production refuses to boot with the 3-minute module.
# RECOVERY_SERVICE_URL=
# RECOVERY_SWEEP_MS=60000

# --- Uniswap v3 liquidity (LIQUIDITY_PROVIDER=dex) ---------------------------
# The only venue that can be executed against on a testnet: Bebop has no
# testnet and CoW has no Base Sepolia, so both can only ever run with real
# money on a mainnet. Defaults below are the Base Sepolia deployments, verified
# with eth_getCode. Override per chain.
# DEX_FACTORY=0x4752ba5DBc23f44D87826276BF6Fd6b1C372aD24
# DEX_ROUTER=0x94cC0AaC535CCDB3C01d6787D6413C739ae12bc4
# DEX_QUOTER=0xC5290058841028F1614F3A6F0F5816cAd0df5E27
# DEX_POSITION_MANAGER=0x27F971cb582BF9E50F397e4d29a5C7A34f11faA2
# Fee tiers probed; the DEEPEST pool wins, not the first that exists.
# DEX_FEE_TIERS=100,500,3000,10000
# Slippage floor written into the swap as amountOutMinimum.
# DEX_SLIPPAGE_BPS=50
# How far the pool's implied EUR/USD may sit from the independent live mid
# before we refuse. This is what separates a pool from a market maker: a maker
# quotes a price it will honour, a pool is wherever the last trade left it and
# anyone can move a thin one. Widening this is not a way to make a bad quote
# work.
# DEX_MAX_MID_DEVIATION_BPS=300
# npm run dex:setup only: which pool to create, and how much of each side.
# DEX_SETUP_FEE=500
# DEX_SEED_EUR=5

# --- Gnosis Pay (permissionless card) ----------------------------------------
# NO API KEY EXISTS for permissionless mode — the user signs in with SIWE and
# Gnosis Pay issues them a JWT. So this works out of the box and the defaults
# below are the real ones; there is nothing to obtain first.
#
# The JWT is never stored by Zold. It is a bearer credential for the user's own
# Gnosis Pay account: the browser holds it for a session and sends it per
# request, and the API forwards and forgets. Only status (connected address,
# Safe address, their KYC state) is persisted.
#
# SIWE_CHAIN_ID is 100 (Gnosis Chain) and is NOT derived from TRANSF_CHAIN_ID.
# Gnosis Pay's account and card Safe live on their chain wherever Zold runs, and
# a SIWE message naming our chain is rejected by them.
# GNOSIS_PAY_BASE_URL=https://api.gnosispay.com
# GNOSIS_PAY_SIWE_CHAIN_ID=100
# GNOSIS_PAY_JWT_TTL_SECONDS=3600
# GNOSIS_PAY_TIMEOUT_MS=12000
# PARTNER MODE ONLY — leave unset. It brings webhooks and card-activity
# attribution, neither of which we have; sending an id we were not issued would
# claim a relationship that does not exist.
# GNOSIS_PAY_PARTNER_ID=

# --- Custody -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Every transfer records the custody mode it ran in (transfer.custody), because
# "we never hold client funds" used to be an emergent property of which venue
# was configured, whether Bridge was live, and whether a venue call happened to
# succeed — and a silent fallback changed the answer with nothing in the record.
#
# REQUIRE_NON_CUSTODIAL=1 turns the preference into a refusal: a transfer that
# would route the sender's funds through the orchestrator is refused at creation
# with the cause named, rather than quietly falling back to it.
#
# NOT on by default, deliberately: with BRIDGE_LIVE unset there is no external
# deposit address for the batch to deliver into, so the output has nowhere to go
# but the orchestrator. Defaulting the refusal on would brick every dry-run and
# testnet deployment. The PATH is non-custodial by default; the GUARANTEE is an
# explicit operator choice, and a deployment moving real money should make it.
# REQUIRE_NON_CUSTODIAL=1

# --- Best execution across venues --------------------------------------------
# LIQUIDITY_PROVIDER=best quotes every venue below in parallel and takes the
# largest out for the same in. Add `rfq` once Bebop supports the token.
# LIQUIDITY_PROVIDER=best
# LIQUIDITY_VENUES=lifi,dex
# Add `rfq` once you hold a Bebop API key: Monerium streams firm EURe quotes
# into Bebop as a market maker (issuer-led, so no intermediary spread), but
# only on ETHEREUM today — set BEBOP_CHAIN=ethereum with it.
# LIQUIDITY_VENUES=lifi,dex,rfq
# BEBOP_CHAIN=ethereum
# Who keeps positive slippage. Default `user`, deliberately: the receipt reports
# marginBps MEASURED between the live mid and what we deliver, so keeping
# surplus quietly would make that number understate what we take. `treasury`
# still records the amount so it can be disclosed rather than hidden.
# LIQUIDITY_SURPLUS_POLICY=user

# --- LI.FI (production venue) -------------------------------------------------
# LIFI_BASE_URL=https://li.quest
# LIFI_API_KEY=
# LIFI_SLIPPAGE=0.005
# LIFI_CHAIN_ID=8453

# --- Testnet faucet -----------------------------------------------------------
# EURe granted to each newly deployed passkey Safe from the deployer wallet, so
# a fresh test account has something to send. Unset/0 disables. The transfer is
# a plain ERC-20 send, so the funding scanner shows it in Activity like any
# deposit. Refused outright in production mode and on every chain where EURe is
# real money (ethereum/gnosis/polygon/base/arbitrum/linea), regardless of this
# value. The deployer needs EURe (the grant) and a little ETH (gas) on the app
# chain; a dry deployer skips with a warning instead of failing onboarding.
# TESTNET_FAUCET_EUR=50
